No, the former (glibc) is the standard C library + GNU and linux
extensions.  It's not _absolutely_ required but without it you don't
have standard C functions (malloc, strlen, etc) unless you use an
alternate libc.

The latter (glib) is a somewhat higher level library, but it is not for
writing apps in X.  Reread my message. The point is that if you write C
code, glib is a very useful library for non-GUI programs.  It gives you
simple APIs for lots of interesting data structures (hash tables,
balanced binary trees and general n-ary trees, single and doubly linked
lists, etc) and gives you higher-level platform independent access to
threading, network socket access, memory allocation, time/date
functions, dynamic library loading, etc.  Nothing GUI related at all.

There are several GUI libraries that _use_ glib (including libgdk,
libgtk, and libgnome, each progressively higher level), and glib
originally came out of the GTK project (but predates GNOME by a long
time).  But it is not at all a GUI library and is worth looking at for
anyone doing C coding even of system-level daemons and such.

It's far closer to the apache portable runtime than to anything GUI
related.



